  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 329 AM CDT Sat Sep 26 2026\n\nAt 500mb, broad ridging across the central CONUS remains in place \nwith the Nor'easter off of the east coast and another trough over \nthe Pacific NW. Shortwaves off of the PNW trough however have helped \nto disturb the ridge over the Midwest, providing lift for the \nshowers we have seen. Near the surface, continuing moist, WAA \nadvection from the south from the surface low over central KS \ninteracts with the shortwaves aloft, supporting rain showers. Showers \nwill be on and off throughout the day Saturday but will increase in \ncoverage by Saturday night into Sunday morning as southerly flow \nincreases along with another upper-level shortwave moving through \nthe region. Instability also increases throughout the day Saturday \nsupporting a 20-30% chance for thunderstorms across the area. \nHowever CAPE values remain low (300-600 J/kg) and shear is limited \n(20-25kts) so severe potential remains low.   \n\nModels have been consistent run-to-run with the ridge moving back \ninto MO Sunday afternoon and the 25th-75th ensemble spread for 500mb \nheights is minimal so there is increasing confidence rain will start \nto move out Sunday afternoon and we should have a dry start to the \nwork week. With the ridge moving back in Monday high temperatures \nwill be back in the mid to upper 70s. \n\nRain chances return for the middle of next week. Another mid-level \ntrough moves in from the Pacific NW towards the SW CONUS where it \nmay combine with the tropical system as it moves into the SW CONUS. \nThis system would bring a large influx of moisture to the area, \nresulting in anomalously high PWAT values for this time of year. With \nsuch high PWAT values we will need to monitor the potential for \nflooding next week. However, there is still uncertainty whether the \ntropical system will cross the Sierra Madre mountains in Mexico, \nwhich would result in a slightly drier setup for next week so we \nwill continue to watch how this system progresses.",
